# Convergence

REQ-01 → D-01/D-02 → T001 → inventory.json and local recovery.
REQ-02 → D-03 → T002 → scripts/automate.py and behavioral contract tests.
REQ-03 → D-04/D-05 → T003/T004 → authorities, exact succession and governance checks.

The inventory read all 92 regular files recursively, including dot entries; no
symlinks or archives were present. 90 unused files removed; README and entry paths
rewritten. Internal imports/calls are retained per file; external active-reference
search found no preexisting Ox callers. Generic Rust wrappers were considered but
consolidated; reusable Python core was evaluated and replaced rather than kept
without consumers. Foreign security policies and licenses are recoverable with all
original bytes. No imported implementation remains in the new entry.

Recovery: /tmp/ox-scripts-integration/recovery; manifest.json hashes all imported
bytes, write-set.json identifies initial existing edit inputs, git-status.txt
records initial dirty/untracked names. Additional affected test preimages are there.
Authority/evidence preimages are also in docs/validation/scripts-task-closure/before.
Removal checked both active and recovered bytes before unlinking; no unrelated
product sources changed. Restore selectively after comparing newer content.

Historical evidence affected by changed authority inputs was archived verbatim in
historical_evidence fields and exact preimages; dependent criteria are INCONCLUSIVE,
not reused approval. affected-evidence.json lists the 27 affected features. Their
bounded successor acceptance is pending; unchanged operational observations remain.
This delivery does not globally approve or requalify those features.

No additional external solution is necessary: Python stdlib and existing Rust
prerequisites suffice. No erase.md, installs, hooks, publication or commits.
Six graphical tests remain omitted by Cargo's normal suite; graphical acceptance
is NOT APPLICABLE to this governance change. Any future UI task must select its
separate X11/native Wayland method. The smoke Rust test initially failed under
socket restrictions, and the identical offline locked suite passed with the local
socket permission outside that sandbox. No product correction was made.

Full suite runs during input stabilization exposed missing copy fixtures/links and
registry changes while older processes held imported predecessor code. Those logs
are retained; only a stable-input successor run can support acceptance.

